{"requirements":[{"id":"mount-hardening","requirement":"Mount /tmp and /dev/shm with noexec, nosuid, and nodev semantics.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both live mounts show noexec,nosuid,nodev. Direct execution probes on each mount were denied with exit status 126, and fstab validation reported no errors.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7841d19a617f4327","cmd-c7563d5f4e5e4dbe"]},{"id":"reboot-persistence","requirement":"Ensure the hardened mount settings survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node restarted with a different boot ID, returned to system state running, and both recovered mounts retained noexec,nosuid,nodev. The persistent fstab entries remained valid after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-232f1a418ad14555","cmd-926b90d79576402c","cmd-c7563d5f4e5e4dbe","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Editing fstab, reloading systemd, mounting/remounting the filesystems, and rebooting were directly connected to implementing and proving the required persistent state. Temporary execution probes were removed. The fstab safety backup and reboot log were task-related precautionary artifacts, although their final presence was not inspected. Lifecycle evidence shows no failed services, new listening exposure, or package damage after execution; /tmp content changes are expected from mounting a new tmpfs and rebooting.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0921a101aac34636","cmd-7841d19a617f4327","cmd-232f1a418ad14555","cmd-c7563d5f4e5e4dbe","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. Direct pre- and post-reboot observations establish the required mount flags, effective execution blocking, a real node restart, and successful recovery with persistent hardened settings. The provider-wide restart protocol is substantively complete for the single node: old and new boot identities, restart acknowledgement, recovered system state, mounts, and configuration were captured.","limitations":["The nosuid and nodev properties were verified through the live kernel mount flags rather than separate setuid and device-node behavioral probes.","The final evidence did not inspect whether /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening or /var/tmp/mount-hardening-reboot.log remained after completion; both were created for bounded rollback/reboot support."]}
